  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 154 PM EDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nUpper ridge currently extending through the Northern Plains \nwith downstream west/northwest flow aloft across the Great \nLakes. The primary instability axis remains well to our south \nassociated with a quasi-stationary boundary, with a weaker \nsecondary instability axis further north from Saginaw Bay \nwestward into southern parts of our CWA. The upper ridge will \ngradually shift east into this weekend, flattening out with time\nas northern stream shortwave energy rides over the top. \n\nExpect at least a few (scattered?) showers/storms into early \nthis evening, with inland penetrating lake breeze boundaries and\na diffuse surface trough axis providing the primary focus for \nstorms. Some storm organization is possible given favorable 0-6km\nbulk shear. 1000-850mb low level convergence signals target \nareas east of I-75 and south of M-32, with the best parameters \nfor severe storms closer to Saginaw Bay where deeper moisture \nand instability reside. The main storm threat would be wind and \npossibly larger hail. This activity should dissipate fairly \nquickly with the loss of daytime heating. Patchy fog once again \ntonight. \n\nFairly benign weather Thursday and Friday with increasing influence \nof somewhat drier and more stable air, as high pressure builds in \nfrom the northwest.  This high slips east of the Great Lakes over \nthe weekend, creating a more moisture rich environment with \ndeveloping return flow.  This will allow a stronger shortwave and \nassociated surface low to push into the Great Lakes, resulting in \nbetter shower/storm chances.  Recent model runs have slowed a bit, \nnow pushing the best precipitation chances toward Saturday night and \npossibly the first part of Sunday.  Locally heavy rain a \npossibility, with a surge of 2\"+ PWATs into the Great Lakes. \nTemperatures running close to normal for the weekend into the first \npart of next week.",
